{
  "meta": {
    "title": "Comb Jelly Wake: Mnemiopsis Carbon Pulse in the Black Sea",
    "date": "2026-11-06",
    "lesson": "The invasive ctenophore Mnemiopsis leidyi devastated Black Sea fisheries in the 1980s-90s by stripping the water of zooplankton. But every comb jelly that dies releases a slug of gelatinous carbon that bacteria scavenge almost instantly. This scenario puts you in that jelly-fall plume: protein-rich mucus and gel particles dominate, copiotrophic bacteria bloom fast on the pulse, and the free-floating phage population tracks them closely, because dense short-lived blooms on gelatinous carbon are exactly where kill-the-winner phage dynamics run hottest. The lesson: a single invasive species' die-off can restructure the microbial loop of an entire sea, turning predation pressure into a bacterial feast.",
    "citation": "Kideys 2002 Science; Tinta et al. 2012 'Bacterial response to jellyfish degradation'",
    "difficulty": "normal",
    "realWorldBasis": "Black Sea Mnemiopsis leidyi jelly-fall carbon flux and copiotroph bacterial bloom dynamics"
  },
